    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go into a bit more detail about the transition from the initial cellular damage to the actual production of the gas? Specifically, how does the syn-propanethial S-oxide interact with the aqueous layer of the eye to create that sudden, sharp stinging feeling? I want to make sure I can explain the 'attack' on the eyes clearly to the students.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the cells are damaged, the release of enzymes like alliinase initiates a cascade, quickly producing syn-propanethial S-oxide, which is highly volatile. This gas diffuses rapidly into the lacrimal fluid, the watery layer of the eye. Upon contact with the moisture, the gas undergoes rapid hydrolysis or oxidation, forming reactive sulfur compounds. These compounds then act as irritants on the sensitive nerve endings in the eyes, triggering the acute stinging sensation.",
      "source": "target",
